regions where the third coordinate does the rescuing: 4
of those, comparisons whose spread difference is measurable: 0

and the same question asked of every arm pair, so the answer is not
about one arm
  arm pairs across all regions: 90
  pairs whose IQR difference excludes zero: 58 (64.4%)

reading
  None of the comparisons the rescue depends on has a measurable
  spread difference, so the rescue is an artifact of an estimate and
  the third coordinate should be dropped. `97` section 10's two-arm
  finding then stands unqualified and p1b's rescue is withdrawn.

  What this does NOT establish: that the spread of a bench arm is a
  thing a consumer should weigh. It establishes only that the arms
  differ on it by more than the estimate's own noise. Whether tail
  behaviour belongs in the coordinate set is a design question and this
  probe does not touch it.
